WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.520
If you want to understand&nbsp;
America today, look at Wisconsin.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.320
Now, when I say Wisconsin, you might be&nbsp;
thinking of a place with lots of cheese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.000
hats made to look like cheese, and, oh yeah,&nbsp;
maybe those World Champions, the Milwaukee Bucks.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.960
But Wisconsin is more than that. Wisconsin is a&nbsp;
SYMBOL of what has happened to the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.480
over the last 50 years. It is a canary in the coal&nbsp;
mine of American life. Whether it’s an assault on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.440
the rights of workers, preventing the majority&nbsp;
of voters from having their say, or assaulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.200
the right to vote itself, Wisconsin has been a&nbsp;
PROVING GROUND for how our country is failing.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.440
But it wasn’t always this way. In fact&nbsp;
it used to be, well…almost the reverse.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:55.680
You see, the German and Scandinavian immigrants&nbsp;
who came to Wisconsin in the nineteenth century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.160
brought with them communitarian ideas. They&nbsp;
had an emphasis on collective solidarity over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.880
individual success. Wisconsinites displayed&nbsp;
a remarkably durable social fabric built on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.760
strong communal ties, vigorous labor unions,&nbsp;
and a deeply democratic, egalitarian worldview.

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.400
Early on, they wanted to abolish slavery&nbsp;
and embraced Lincoln’s Republican Party.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.120
Later, they embraced populist,&nbsp;
anti-monopolist movements like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.160
Progressive Party of Robert La Follette. And in&nbsp;
the twentieth century, in cities like Milwaukee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.000
they elected socialist mayors and&nbsp;
socialist members of Congress.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.840
They saw Wisconsin as a “LABORATORY OF DEMOCRACY,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.960
a place where ideas like the progressive income&nbsp;
tax or unemployment insurance were first tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.640
before being implemented on a national scale. It&nbsp;
was a concept they called “THE WISCONSIN IDEA”:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.720
that government was a tool to better the&nbsp;
lives of ordinary people. There was a broad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.720
social democratic consensus, backed by a powerful&nbsp;
labor movement, about what government can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.160
and should provide for ordinary people.&nbsp;
That is what defined Wisconsin for decades.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.640
But in the second half of the twentieth&nbsp;
century, all of that came under assault.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.920
Starting in the 1970s and accelerating in the&nbsp;
‘80s and ‘90s, waves of deindustrialization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.480
swept across the Midwest, and they&nbsp;
hit Wisconsin particularly hard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.320
The industrial base that supported so much&nbsp;
of social and political life decayed rapidly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.600
as companies outsourced or&nbsp;
automated their workforces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.080
factories were shuttered, workers&nbsp;
were laid off, and towns fell apart.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.840
It was a disaster for places like Milwaukee&nbsp;
or Kenosha, where deindustrialization led to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.160
crisis of joblessness and despair that lingers&nbsp;
on today. In today’s Wisconsin, 1 in every 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.160
children live in poverty. That includes 1&nbsp;
out of 2 Black children living in poverty.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.960
And in the 1980s, a similar crisis&nbsp;
began in Wisconsin’s rural areas.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.360
As interest rates climbed, family farmers found&nbsp;
themselves unable to pay back their debts, leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.880
to a massive campaign of farm repossessions where&nbsp;
HUNDREDS OF THOUSANDS of people lost everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.840
This farm crisis led not only to&nbsp;
hundreds of suicides by farmers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.680
but also to a transformation of agriculture:&nbsp;
family farmers were pushed out, and huge new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.800
agribusiness giants like Conagra or Cargill&nbsp;
took over much of Wisconsin’s farmland.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.880
After the two successive crises, Wisconsin,&nbsp;
like much of the United States, found itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.720
in a quiet but deep social malaise&nbsp;
by the beginning of the twenty-first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.520
century: a despair that fed off a very real&nbsp;
experience of decay. Median incomes were stagnant;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:47.360
incarceration was rising; factories were closing,&nbsp;
rural towns depopulating, atomization and suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:53.360
rising. The only people doing very well were&nbsp;
members of a new class of ultra-rich elites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.360
the same people who ran those&nbsp;
companies like Cargill or Conagra.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:03.440
But political leaders in both parties didn’t care.&nbsp;
To them, Wisconsin was still a testing ground for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:10.400
new ideas. But those ideas had changed. Government&nbsp;
was no longer a tool that could help people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.880
but instead an enemy to be&nbsp;
slashed, limited, made ineffective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.440
Republicans focused mainly on catering to the&nbsp;
new elites, while Democrats gradually abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.840
their relationship with labor unions&nbsp;
and focused on educated professionals.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.200
Under Republican governor Tommy&nbsp;
Thompson in the ‘80s and ‘90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:35.200
Wisconsin pioneered “welfare reform” programs&nbsp;
that reduced benefits and cut eligibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.400
just as people needed them most. And under&nbsp;
Democratic governor Jim Doyle in the 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.080
the state eagerly cut state budgets&nbsp;
and laid off government workers.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.200
None of this, of course, improved material&nbsp;
conditions. Plants were continuing to close or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.800
downsize, and cities and rural areas continued to&nbsp;
decay. And in 2010, amidst a wave of discontent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.200
Republicans won the state legislature and&nbsp;
the governor’s office – winning about a third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.160
of union households. The new governor&nbsp;
was a conservative named Scott Walker.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:09.840
Walker was very different from early&nbsp;
generations who had defined Wisconsin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.440
He liked to play up his blue-collar credentials,&nbsp;
and occasionally drove a Harley-Davidson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.680
motorcycle. But beneath that facade, the new&nbsp;
governor was an agent of the wealthy elites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.560
And he saw his time as governor as an&nbsp;
opportunity to put their worldview into practice.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.040
The main enemy, Walker thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.360
was Wisconsin’s labor unions. Though they&nbsp;
were much weaker than they had once been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.440
they were still the biggest remnant of the&nbsp;
social democratic coalition that was once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.120
central to Wisconsin politics. So the governor&nbsp;
adopted a strategy of “divide and conquer.”

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.200
First, he sought to isolate the state’s public&nbsp;
sector unions, branding them as greedy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:54.320
wasteful of taxpayer funds. He enacted a law&nbsp;
outlawing collective bargaining for workplace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:00.960
safety, capping wage increases, and requiring&nbsp;
recertification elections every year. Within a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.720
few years, Wisconsin’s public sector unions&nbsp;
lost about 70 PERCENT of their membership.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:12.160
And once he had hamstrung the public sector&nbsp;
unions, he focused on private unions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.400
signing a right-to-work bill that aimed&nbsp;
to dramatically reduce union membership.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:23.120
Within a few years, Walker had cut Wisconsin’s&nbsp;
unionization rate nearly in HALF – to roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:30.240
the same rate as Alabama, a state with a long and&nbsp;
violent anti-union history. In just five years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.720
Walker had successfully hobbled the&nbsp;
state’s long tradition of labor power.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:41.600
At the same time, the governor led a dramatic&nbsp;
remaking of democracy in Wisconsin. Shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:47.440
taking office, he approved a new electoral map of&nbsp;
the state that divided districts to ensure large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:52.880
majorities for Republicans in every legislative&nbsp;
election: Democratic voters were divided into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.680
a few districts where they’d win overwhelmingly,&nbsp;
and then a larger number of districts where they’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:02.800
always lose. It was one of the most effective&nbsp;
gerrymanders in American political history.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:11.200
In 2018, for instance, Democrats won 53 percent&nbsp;
of the statewide votes for State Assembly to 45&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.800
percent for Republicans. But Republicans still&nbsp;
won about two-thirds of State Assembly seats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.400
In order to have gained even a small majority&nbsp;
in 2018, Democrats would have needed to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.000
by about 20 points statewide, 65 percent&nbsp;
of the vote – an almost impossible feat.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.280
With an essentially permanent majority,&nbsp;
Republicans are empowered in Wisconsin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.960
government basically no matter what happens in&nbsp;
any election. And when a Democratic governor won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:45.440
office in 2018, the Republican legislature&nbsp;
executed an eleventh-hour power-grab that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.400
stripped him of his powers and strengthened&nbsp;
their own. Because Wisconsin doesn’t have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.200
system of direct democracy, there’s practically&nbsp;
nothing that can be done about any of it.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:00.480
Wisconsin today is a very different place&nbsp;
than the Wisconsin of a hundred years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.040
The egalitarian worldview of the&nbsp;
Wisconsin progressives and socialists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:10.960
is gone. The institutions that allowed that world&nbsp;
to flourish – the factories and labor unions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:16.720
the thriving towns and cities, the basic sense&nbsp;
of COMMUNITY and SOLIDARITY – have disintegrated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.640
And the democracy that was once&nbsp;
the heart of the “Wisconsin Idea”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.640
has become a farce, where every election&nbsp;
returns the same party to power.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.800
What this means for Wisconsinites is&nbsp;
that policies that are popular with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:36.000
the vast majority of Wisconsin voters&nbsp;
– legalizing recreational mairjuana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.960
raising the minimum wage, expanding&nbsp;
Medicaid – have no shot of becoming law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.880
In other words, Wisconsinites don’t&nbsp;
live in a representative democracy.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.200
But this isn’t just a story about Wisconsin.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:55.040
It is, in part, a global story, the same&nbsp;
story that has played out all across the West:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:58.480
in the north of France, in the east&nbsp;
of Germany, or in northern England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.000
It’s a story of loss: not just the loss of&nbsp;
jobs and the dissolution of communities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:10.000
but of the loss of possibility, of hope –&nbsp;
the loss of the bright futures once offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.960
by the mass politics of working people.&nbsp;
All we are left with is a dreary present.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.080
And there is no better symbol of that&nbsp;
than Wisconsin. A state that, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:26.800
always imperfect, once represented equality,&nbsp;
solidarity, and democracy has become a SYMBOL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.160
of a NEW TYPE of society. We traded away&nbsp;
those time-tested ideals – and in turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.320
got a society that was far&nbsp;
more oligarchic, more atomized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:41.840
more adrift than the one that had come before.&nbsp;
And in that bargain, we are ALL worse off.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.160
I’m Philip Rocco, associate&nbsp;
professor of political science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.840
at Marquette University in Milwaukee,&nbsp;
Wisconsin, for the Gravel Institute.

